1. CAST
    CAST(expression AS target_data_type)
    e.g: CAST(measurement_time AS DATE) -> Change 2026-08-02 14:30:00 into 2026-08-02
    CAST('123' AS INTEGER) -> Cast '123' into 123 integer
    CAST(5 AS FLOAT) / 2 ->> Return 2.5 instead of 2
    CAST(2026 AS VARCHAR) ->> Cast 2026 into "2026"

2. Comparision between FILTER and CASE WHEN
    AGG_FUNCTION(column_name) FILTER (WHERE condition)  = AGG_FUNCTION(CASE WHEN condition THEN column_name ELSE 0 END)
    e.g: SUM(measurement_value) FILTER (WHERE measurement_num % 2 != 0) AS odd_sum = SUM(CASE WHEN measurement_num % 2 != 0 THEN measurement_value ELSE 0 END) AS odd_sum

3. JOIN 
    INNER JOIN: only the rows that have matching values in both tables, use when you care about records that exist in both places
    LEFT JOIN: Returns all rows from the left table, plus matched rows from the right table. Unmatched rows from the right table will show as NULL
    CROSS JOIN: Combines every row of the first table with every row of the second table (M rows x N rows).

4. LEAD() and LAG(): Both LEAD() and LAG() are positional window functions used to access data from other rows in a result set without performing a self-join.
    LEAD(): Looks FORWARD to pull values from subsequent (future) rows.
    LAG(): Looks BACKWARD to pull values from preceding (past) rows.

    e.g: LEAD(transaction_date, 1) OVER(PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY transaction_date) AS second_day,
        LEAD(transaction_date, 2) OVER(PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY transaction_date) AS third_day

5. MODE() WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY target_column [ASC | DESC])
    It counts how many times each value appears in target_column and returns the value that appears most frequently.If there is a tie , 
    it picks the first value according to the ORDER BY direction specified.

    e.g: SELECT MODE() WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY product_category) AS most_frequent_category FROM sales;
